01_Port Security

Why we did this
================
In a Layer 2 network, any device can be connected to an active switch port and start communicating on the network.
To prevent unauthorized devices from connecting to the network, Cisco introduced Port Security.
Port Security allows the switch to limit the number of MAC addresses learned on a port and specify which devices are allowed.

Example:
A company assigns a PC to Switch Port Fa0/1. An employee disconnects the company PC and connects a personal laptop. Without Port Security, the personal laptop will gain network access.
Port Security ensures only authorized MAC addresses can use the port.

What will happen
================
1. Switch learns allowed MAC address(es).
2. If an authorized device connects, normal traffic is allowed.
3. If an unauthorized device connects, a security violation occurs.
4. Based on configured violation mode, the switch can:
   - Drop traffic
   - Log the violation
   - Disable the port

Advantages and Disadvantages of this, hence what came next
==========================================================
Advantages
----------
1. Prevents unauthorized devices from connecting.
2. Protects against MAC flooding attacks.
3. Simple and easy to configure.
4. Reduces insider threats.
5. Increases access-layer security.

Disadvantages
-------------
1. Manual MAC address management can be difficult.
2. Device replacement requires configuration changes.
3. Does not authenticate users.
4. Does not protect against spoofed MAC addresses.
5. Not scalable for large enterprise environments.

Hence what came next
--------------------
Because manually configuring MAC addresses was difficult, Cisco introduced Sticky MAC Security.
Sticky MAC Security automatically learns and saves MAC addresses, reducing administrative effort.

One-Shot Configuration
======================

Switch(config)# interface fa0/1
Switch(config-if)# switchport mode access
Switch(config-if)# switchport port-security
Switch(config-if)# switchport port-security maximum 1
Switch(config-if)# switchport port-security mac-address 0011.2233.4455
Switch(config-if)# switchport port-security violation shutdown
Switch(config-if)# end

Verification
============
show port-security
show port-security interface fa0/1
show port-security address

Expected Result
===============
Only MAC address 0011.2233.4455 can use Fa0/1.
If any other device is connected:
- Security violation is generated.
- Port goes into Err-Disabled state.
- User loses connectivity until the port is recovered.

Summary
=======
Problem:
Anyone could connect to an active switch port.

Solution:
Port Security restricts which MAC addresses are allowed on a switch port.

Limitation:
Managing MAC addresses manually is difficult.

